#step("ground-state ⟨x²⟩ double integration by parts", 4, [$I = L^3/(2 pi^2)$, so $⟨ x^2 ⟩ = L^2( ((1)/(3)) - ((1)/(2 pi^2)) )$; with $⟨ x ⟩ = L/2$ gives $Delta x = L sqrt(1/12 - 1/(2 pi^2)) approx 0.18L$])
#step("ground-state ⟨x²⟩ double integration by parts", 5, [$Delta x dot.c Delta p approx 0.57 planck$, above the $planck/2$ floor since only a Gaussian saturates])
